1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,100
Hello, this is Yamyam Coding. Fishing,
futuristic racing,

2
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:12,440
and predicting a god's next attack: AI helped
make these games, too.

3
00:00:12,940 --> 00:00:17,600
We'll look at six games and the development
work their creators disclosed.

4
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:21,040
Each game gets a brief summary of its
development environment and tools,

5
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:25,220
while we distinguish early prototypes from
ongoing projects.

6
00:00:25,620 --> 00:00:29,280
Let's begin on this little island, where you
can see beneath the water.

7
00:00:29,570 --> 00:00:36,900
I Just Wanted to Fish lets you explore an
island and fish. A character casts from the

8
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:42,360
beach, with fish and coral visible in the
shallows. Small crabs wander along the shore.

9
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:45,560
The creator reported almost three weeks of
work on this game preparing for

10
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:50,860
a Steam release. That is a development
milestone, not a completed release.

11
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:54,780
It uses Unity, with Opus and Blender
contributing to production.

12
00:00:55,340 --> 00:01:01,760
The creator designed the terrain and layout,
supplied references,

13
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:06,560
and repeatedly revised the models and
movement. The character, water and

14
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:11,020
environment had to work together. The Steam
disclosure says no AI is used during play.

15
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:13,720
While this is a PC fishing game,

16
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:17,660
the next example is a small world you can
enter through a browser.

17
00:01:18,440 --> 00:01:22,480
This is Emberfall — Luminati. The characters
resemble pixel art, while the houses and

18
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:28,980
paths can be viewed from different camera
angles. It feels like a miniature village.

19
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:32,560
It runs in a browser and is built on Three.js.

20
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:36,220
The game player and level editor have separate
addresses. You can move the character

21
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:40,220
and rotate the camera in the game, while the
editor handles terrain height,

22
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:46,420
objects and lighting. Both links will be
labeled in the description.

23
00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:50,760
The exact AI model version and total
development duration remain unconfirmed. From

24
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:56,920
the appearance of a small world, let's move to
one that keeps adding story and systems.

25
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:00,920
Nightshoal also features fishing and
exploration, but with a different atmosphere.

26
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:05,260
A boat travels along waterways between cliffs
and forests.

27
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:09,820
It uses Godot, with AI tools divided among
code, graphics and sound.

28
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:14,680
A Windows public build is available, and the
creator's update marks week eleven.

29
00:02:15,140 --> 00:02:19,100
Quests, story, fishing and inventory systems
are still being expanded.

30
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:24,280
The creator continues revising the map and
features while integrating work from several

31
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:29,420
tools. From filling a world, let's move to
racing that responds rapidly to the player.

32
00:02:29,940 --> 00:02:34,600
In VECTOR: Anti-Gravity Racing, floating craft
navigate corners,

33
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:37,840
cross boost sections and compete for position.

34
00:02:37,889 --> 00:02:41,540
It is a Three.js browser game developed
through AI-assisted coding.

35
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,120
The creator describes using WebGPU, with a
WebGL 2 fallback where necessary.

36
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:55,360
Both provide browser graphics functions. AI
was also asked to use Blender for the models.

37
00:02:55,720 --> 00:03:00,080
The exact development duration and AI model
version could not be confirmed.

38
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:05,660
Our next game takes the opposite approach to
speed: pausing time to consider each move.

39
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:10,260
AUGURY is a duel between gods where time
pauses as you choose an action.

40
00:03:10,620 --> 00:03:13,880
A green silhouette previews a move, and
committing advances the result.

41
00:03:14,180 --> 00:03:19,500
Predicting the opponent's next action is
central.

42
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:23,340
It uses Phaser, with DreamLayer among the
tools used for images.

43
00:03:23,780 --> 00:03:29,260
You can play directly on a web page. The
development record describes removing image

44
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:34,160
backgrounds, preparing sizes and colors, and
adjusting body and prop layers and movement.

45
00:03:34,540 --> 00:03:37,460
Generated pictures became readable game
characters.

46
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:41,360
The total development duration and exact model
versions remain unconfirmed.

47
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:46,680
That refinement work is worth remembering when
we look at the next short milestone.

48
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:51,260
Rally Road Rash features dirt-track
collisions, jumps and rockets.

49
00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:57,400
Its creator reported building an initial
version in about three days.

50
00:03:57,860 --> 00:04:01,660
It was made in Sandscape, with work divided
between Opus and Astra.

51
00:04:02,100 --> 00:04:05,700
The game runs in a browser. The creator
assigned vehicles and physics to Opus

52
00:04:05,700 --> 00:04:11,760
and visual refinement to Astra, while using
Meshy for major vehicles and props.

53
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:16,520
The creator edited the levels personally and
reported twenty-five years of experience.

54
00:04:16,580 --> 00:04:22,360
Motorcycle physics were still being refined.
Three days describes that initial version.

55
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:24,600
Together, the six games reveal a change more
clearly

56
00:04:25,020 --> 00:04:28,020
than their development-time numbers alone.

57
00:04:28,540 --> 00:04:32,660
AI contributions extend beyond code to models,
images, movement and sound.

58
00:04:32,660 --> 00:04:38,160
Some of the resulting games are available to
interact with directly.

59
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:44,520
Even under the same 'AI game' label,

60
00:04:44,580 --> 00:04:49,200
creators selected and adapted generated work
in different ways.

61
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:51,560
Looking at what they made and how they refined
it reveals more than tool names alone.

62
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:56,300
This report is based on public material
checked on October 18, 2026. Game links and

63
00:04:56,740 --> 00:05:02,800
development records will be in the
description. Which one would you like to try?
